hat they could view insight  discovery as a problem break that  problem down into manageable components  and then start working towards a  solution and so in this talk we're going  to look at  an example of a real data set I'm going  to introduce you to the framework that I  use and then I'm gonna walk you through  applying those concepts from that  framework to that example data set but  before I jump right into that I'd like  to talk a little bit about how I think  about data and provide a little bit of  context so data is essentially encoded  information and the world that we live  in is a very complex web of it each of  us has a ton of information and details  floating around in our heads there's  constantly new information coming in and  things are constantly changing and so as  humans we have this ability and this  capacity to deal with all this  complexity and all this information that  we're constantly receiving and the way  that we do that is by putting things in  order we group things into categories we  create hierarchical classifications  taxonomy x' ontology x' all of these  things and these things help to provide  order and structure to the way we view  the world around us and they allow us to  look at something recognize the  similarities it has to a group of other  things quickly identify some of its  properties and then be able to make some  decisions about it  and in fact if we think about it a data  set all it really is is just slice of  this complex information that somebody  has put together to try to communicate  something about this complex world to a  human or to a machine and so the fact  that this ability is built into us is  great right and we use it all the time  without ever really thinking about it  and that includes unfortunately we're  analyzing our data so I wanted to put  together data exploration framework that  leveraged this ability that we have and  that would help us make better use of  this ability in our science workflows  and so here is the framework that I came  up with  it's basically broken down into two  phases there's the prep phase first and  then the exploration phase that comes  after that and you can think of the prep  phase kind of as a ritual you can  technically do the explore phase without  it but it's the prep phase that's going  to allow you to do the exploration  so I highly recommend that you do that  and I'm gonna be walking through this  framework here for the rest of the  presentation so the data set that I'm  gonna be applying this framework to  today is the Environmental Protection  Agency's fuel economy data set and this  is what it looks like after some light  cleanup I've also included the URL on  here where you can get the data set  yourself if you wanted to and so the  first step in the in the prep part of  framework is to identify a couple of  things about our data first is going to  be to identify the types of information  that we have in the data set and so  taking a look at this data there's a few  things that there's a few of these types  of information that are kind of obvious  they they jump right out at us  there's vehicle attribute information  there's vehicle manufacturer information  engine information fuel information  transmission information that kind of  stuff there's also some other types of  information that may not be so obvious  and so because we have the year that  that each of the vehicles was made we  should be able to see changes in the  data over time we also have relationship  data in our data set both in terms of  the relationships between fields and  relationships between entities and our  data and finally because we have both  time and relationship information in  there we can acquire information about  how those relationships have changed  over time the next step here was to  identify the actual entities that are in  our data and so what exactly do I mean  by entities when I refer to an entity  I'm talking about the individual  analyzable units that are in a data set  and so in order to conduct analysis on  the data the entities must be  distinguishable from one another right a  human or a machine needs to be able to  take a look at them and say this is one  this is another this is another and  finally entities usually have a  hierarchical structure whether they're  able to be aggregated into a system or a  higher level  they belong to and so let's take a look  at some examples of entities that are in  this particular data set here to kind of  try to drive this concept home so we're  starting off at level one here which is  the most granular that we have it's the  year and the specific model of the  vehicle at level two we can aggregate  that up to just the year and the model  type which is a little bit less granular  and from there we can we have a few  different directions that we can go we  can go the year and the vehicle class  the year in the math or just strip away  the year and just keep the model type  and then at level four if we want to  continue going we have just the vehicle  classes the years or all the makes and  so to drive this moment and make this a  little clearer here are some like  concrete examples of actual records that  are in the dataset so at level one we're  talking about a 2016 Ford Mustang 2.3  liter v4 engine automatic transmission  rear-wheel drive right so very detailed  at level two we're rolling that up to  say okay all 2016 Ford Mustangs and then  so forth and so on up the hierarchy  there and and again doing this is  important and it'll help you think about  all the different things that you can do  to the data and the different ways you  can look at it later on I see a lot of  people that are new to data science that  don't do this they don't think like this  and so they miss things in the data that  they really should be caching so I hope  that this example makes it a little bit  clearer the next step in the praise in  the prep phase here is to review some  transformation and some of the  visualization methods that we have  available to us so that they're fresh in  our heads and so first up I have here  the transformation methods and so the  first one I have listed here is is  filtering which is basically taking the  data set and making it smaller by either  looking at less rows or less columns or  both the next one is aggregation and  disaggregation and this is where you're  changing the levels that we look  before that you're analyzing the data at  getting either more or less granular  after that we have pivoting which is  where you aggregate by more than one  variable at a time so along each each of  the axes so the rows and the columns  each have a variable and then you look  at some count or some average or  something like that and how those are  grouped and then the last one I have  listed on here is graph transformations  which is where you link the entities  you're analyzing by the things they have  in common and then you examine the  connections between the entities and how  they're related to each other and so by  transforming the data you're altering  the structure of it right which lets you  look at it from from several different  perspectives and just like looking at  anything else from a bunch of different  perspectives you're gonna learn  something new about it from every way  that you look at it and the awesome  thing about this is that the number of  ways that you can transform the data is  only limited by your creativity  essentially and so this for me is one of  the most exciting things about working  with data all the different things that  you can do to it and all the creative  ways that you can transform it in  addition to transforming the data I also  like to go step further and visualize it  sometimes the transformations that you  perform kind of in the earlier step can  be kind of difficult to look at and  interpret visually and plotting them  really allows you to pull in the human  visual cortex into your analytical  process and makes the insights really  jump right out at you and transformation  and visualization really go hand in hand  since there's a multitude of ways to  transform data there's also a multitude  of ways to visualize it as well and so  here I've listed a few of these and  there'll be additional pictures later on  in the presentation if you're not  familiar with a few of these all right  and so the last step in the prep phase  is to create some additional categorical  fields that are going to make our data  easier to transform and explore later on  and we're going to do this  three ways so the first way that we're  going to do this is by identifying  opportunities to create higher level  categories out of the current categories  that we have in our data so I have an  example up here so imagine that you're  aggregating by transmission this dataset  has 44 unique categories for the Tran  transmission field and so that's gonna  be tough to look at and you're gonna  have to work really hard to find  insights there but uh if we take a look  at the different categories that are in  this and we're able to find some things  that they have in common then we can  make it a lot easier on ourselves so in  this case the thing that all 44 values  here have in common is that they either  fall into an automatic transmission  category or a manual transmission  category and so we've created a new  field called transmission type that just  has whether that record falls into one  of those two categories the same thing  for the vehicle class is just another  example this one has 34 unique  categories in that categorical variable  field and I was able to distill those  down into eight categories that are  listed here on the slide and so that  makes things a little more manageable  and so keep in mind that you're not over  writing the original categories you're  just creating another categorical field  that's allowing you to view the  information at a higher level and so if  you need to drill down to get more  granular categories you can always do  that but at least after you do this you  actually have the option the next way  that we can create additional categories  for ourselves is by pinning some of the  continuous variables and breaking them  up into different categories based on  how high or how low they are there's  multiple ways that you can do this but I  like to use quintiles because it gives  me kind of one moderate category in the  middle and then two categories outside  of that that are kind of moderately  higher and lower and then two other  categories at the ends that are kind of  more extreme and I just find it that's a  very intuitive way to to split  and so I've identified four fields in  the data set here on the slide that we  can do this for in the current data set  and then the final thing we're going to  do in the prep phase here is cluster our  data and create different categories out  of the different clusters that result  from that and now there's there's a few  reasons that I like to use clustering to  create categories like this the first is  that it takes multiple fields into  consideration together at the same time  whereas the other categorization  approaches we we were looking at earlier  only consider one field at a time  it also creates some new categories for  you automatically which is great because  it it saves time versus you having to  comb through the data yourself trying to  identify any patterns and form  categories based off of that  the third reason I like clustering is  because it sometimes groups things in  ways that you as a human may not may not  have thought of yourself and I'm a big  fan of kind of humans and-and-and  machines working together on stuff like  this but the question with clustering is  always well how many clusters do I use  and the answer is usually it depends  there's no real right answer for this  now that being said I personally try to  keep it to under ten clusters when I'm  doing this for creating categories  because otherwise it becomes too many  categories for you to intuitively try to  distinguish between and so I usually  start out with about eight or ten  clusters and then I try to work my way  down until I find some clear boundaries  between the clusters so you should be  able to look at them and visually  distinguish one from the other and so  starting out that's going to look like  this so just from looking at this you  can tell that we probably have too many  clusters here because clusters three  four and six look a lot alike clusters  zero and one are also pretty close to  each other and clusters two and five  also look a lot alike as well and so  just judging from the way this looks I'm  willing to bet that we can cut the  number of clusters in half  and get some better distinct  distinctions between each of the  clusters so let's go ahead and do that  and so now here we've reduced the number  of clusters to four and now the clusters  look a lot better right clusters 0 and 1  look like polar opposites of each other  cluster 2 looks like it's pretty well  balanced all across the board and  consider 3 looks like it's kind of  halfway between clusters 0 and cluster 2  and so now that we have a good number of  clusters that's great but it's kind of  hard to remember what clusters 0 1 2 &amp; 3  mean right and so the next thing I like  to do is I to assign some descriptive  names to the clusters based on kind of  how how low or how high each of the  values were each of the boxes so for our  data set I'm gonna assign each of the  clusters the names that I have on the  slide here and now we have these  meaningful categories and now our data  is in really good shape and that means  that we can move on to the explore phase  so in the explore phase we're first  going to look at filtering and  aggregation and then we're going to look  at the relationships between fields and  then the relationships between entities  and we're going to look at these things  visually we're gonna look at these  things over time and we're going to use  them to discover insights in our data  and so here I filtered the data by year  so just for 2016 and I've a graded it by  vehicle category which was one of the  eight categories that we created earlier  and so this is what the data  transformation looks like as well as the  corresponding bar chart and so from this  we can see that small cards there's  significantly more of them than all the  other types of vehicles SUVs are kind of  the next most popular follows followed  by the midsize cars and so once you've  done this it's very easy to change out  either the year or the category that  you're aggregating on to get a different  perspective on the data  so here I've changed out the year and at  aggregated on  same category and so here you'll you'll  notice that SUVs didn't exist yet small  cars were even more popular and pickup  trucks were as well and so in addition  to changing the year as I mentioned you  can also change out the field that  you're grouping on right so here going  back to 2016 here I've changed out the  vehicle category for the engine size  quintiles that we created earlier here's  the fuel of the fuel efficiency  quintiles that we created earlier and  even our our clusters and this is great  but what if we wanted to take a look at  the vehicle manufacturers let's say that  we wanted to know how many models each  manufacturer currently makes or even  which manufacturer currently makes the  most right we can do that pretty easily  as well just change out the make for  whatever the category that you had in  there was and so here we can see that  BMW currently has more models on the  market than at any other auto  manufacturer and by quite a bit actually  okay so so so far we've been looking at  the data at a pretty high level we've  been aggregating by a single categorical  variable at a time next we're going to  go down a level and take a look at two  or more variables at a time and the way  that we do that is with pivot tables so  here I've created a pivot table heat map  where you have a row for each fuel  efficiency category and a column for  each engine size category and you can  see how many vehicles fall into each  combination of these and just like we  did earlier we can slice by a different  year and easily get a different  perspective right so here's what the  same heat map looks like for 1985 right  2016 1985 and with this approach we're  not just limited to two variables we can  have multiple variables for each of the  axes and it'll show us all the different  combinations of values for those  variables by whatever other variables we  want and so in this next heat map I've  basically  doubled up on the rose so we've got it  engine size and fuel efficiency  combinations represented by the rose and  we've added a third variable which is  the vehicle category across each of the  columns and so now we can see a finer  level of detail about what types of cars  of what size engines at what level of  fuel efficiency we've we've added a  little bit more complexity and a little  bit more granularity to how we're  viewing the data here and so if we want  to go back to analyzing the different  auto manufacturers what we can do is we  can take a look at how the number of  models they have on the market currently  is distributed across all the different  vehicle categories and so that's exactly  what I've done here and now you can get  a better sense of each auto company's  current strategy in terms of the breadth  versus the depth of vehicles that they  currently offer so it looks like BMWs  doubling down on small cars as is  Porsche of Ford and Chevy and Nissan are  going for breadth right they make  vehicles in just about every category  Jeep and Land Rover are basically only  creating SUVs Rams only creating pickup  trucks and so forth and so on and so in  addition to looking at these 