milar attributes to each other so  we're gonna examine how the different  manufacturers relate to each other and  how those relationships have changed  over time as well in order to do this  we're gonna have to transform our data  set again from the relational form it's  currently in here into graph form and  that looks kind of like this so we have  two different companies as make X and  make Y and then we have an edge weight  and this edge weight tells us how many  vehicles with similar attributes the two  companies have in common so the more  vehicles with similar attributes they  have in common the more alike the two  companies are and the stronger their  connection is going to be and so looking  at the data in this format you might say  well there's not much to this and you're  right but having it formatted like this  to create really interesting things like  this so this is a network visualization  showing the relationships of all the  different vehicle manufacturers to each  other and from taking a look at this we  can see for example that the companies  that make the most types of vehicles I'm  across the most number of categories are  positioned towards the center of the  network here and that's because they're  gonna have more in common with other  companies just as a result of the depth  and the breadth of their offerings you  can contrast that with the companies  that have more specialized offerings  like Ram at the very top there or Land  Rover at the bottom these companies make  only one kind of vehicle so they have  less connections in their position near  kind of the edges of the network and so  this is great  but looking at all this at once does  kind of make it look a little cluttered  so what I usually like to do from here  is try to filter down and look at an ego  graph for a specific auto manufacturer  so here we have an ego graph for Nissan  and you can basically see here that  they're now at the center of the network  and you can also see all t